                Text {
                    text: "Checked the ordering hazard: log ids are `generate_uuid_v7()` (`crates/agent_session/src/outbound/postgres/mod.rs:247`) and the read is `ORDER BY created_at ASC, id ASC` (line 270). v7 is time-ordered, so the tie-break is chronological, and `created_at` comes from per-statement `now()` at microsecond resolution. Ordering is sound — frames can't reorder. Good, because the recordings pack up to 19 frames into a single millisecond.\n\nThat means your \"missing parts\" case is a small enumerable set, not chaos. Here's what the data says about each:\n\n**Tool call with no response — real, 2 of 46.** Both at lines 296–299 of a 299-line log: the live session. So this isn't corruption, it's the *in-flight* state. It also becomes permanent when a session dies (`SessionStatus::Disconnected` already exists at `agent_session/src/domain/model.rs:32`). Not an error — `ToolStatus::Pending` with no output is a thing you want to render, as a spinner or \"interrupted\".\n\n**Orphan patch (update before open) — 0 of 45.** Only reachable through `session/load` resume, where the log legitimately starts mid-stream. There's one `session/load` in the recordings.\n\n**Unpaired permission request — 0 of 1.** Same story: in-flight, not error.\n\n**Partial open frame — common, and this is the one that actually bites.** The `Write` call opens as `{\"rawInput\":{},\"title\":\"Write\",\"content\":[],\"locations\":[]}`. Every renderable field is absent and arrives later. If the type demands them at construction, this frame fails — and it's *normal traffic*.\n\n## So I'd argue against `Result` here\n\nBecause these messages are derived on every read, a fold error means **the channel renders blank**. Failing is the worst available outcome for a render path — a partially-known tool call is strictly better than nothing. Every case above is legitimately representable:\n\n```rust\nfn fold(log: impl IntoIterator<Item = AgentSessionLog>) -> Fold;\n\npub struct Fold {\n    pub messages: Vec<AgentMessage>,\n    pub anomalies: Vec<Anomaly>,   // observability, not failure\n}\n\npub enum Anomaly {\n    PatchBeforeOpen { tool_call: ToolUseId },\n    UnknownSessionUpdate { kind: String },\n    UnknownToolKind { kind: String },\n}\n```\n\nPush \"missing parts\" into the *type* — optionals inside `ToolUse`, so a call with nothing known still renders as a bare \"used a tool\" row — and surface the weirdness through `anomalies` instead of `Err`.\n\nThe payoff is in testing: your replay tests assert `anomalies.is_empty()` against the recorded fixtures, so protocol drift fails CI loudly, while production degrades gracefully instead of showing an empty channel. You get strictness where it's cheap and leniency where it's expensive.\n\nWant me to write the vocabulary into the crate now, with a `test.rs` that replays the three `.jsonl` files? I'd need your call on the turn-granularity question from before — one `AgentMessage` per turn is still my default.No `Result`, one explicit match. That kills the ports trait too. Let me check the one thing that decides the match's shape — how we get from a log row to typed ACP.",
                },
