                Text {
                    text: "Both changes done. **Hexagonal boundary checked** — audit greps come back clean: `domain/` has zero references to `channels`, axum, sqlx, or `crate::inbound`; `inbound/` does no persistence or infrastructure work. No authz or business policy exists in this crate yet — queries are scoped by `AgentSessionId` only, so access control belongs to whichever inbound surface eventually exposes this, minting a receipt against the session's thread.\n\n**1. The domain no longer knows comms exists.** `inbound/comms_message.rs` is the concrete fold — protocol log in, `channels::domain::models::ChannelMessage` out — and it's the only file in the crate that names a comms type. The domain keeps its own vocabulary (turns, tool calls, permissions) and the coupling sits at the edge.\n\nTwo things worth flagging about that adapter:\n- **It's lossy on purpose.** `ChannelMessage.content` is a `String`, so parts flatten to markdown: tool calls become fenced blocks with status markers, thoughts a blockquote, diffs a `diff` fence. That's the reason the layering pays off — when comms carries structured parts, `render_markdown` is the only thing that changes. It also means ANSI stripping lives *here*, not in the domain, which still preserves the escapes.\n- **Message ids are derived, not generated.** UUIDv5 over `(session, turn, author)`, so re-reading a session yields identical ids. Without that, every poll would look like a fresh batch of messages. There's a test pinning it.\n\n**2. `agent_session` owns the log store.** Deleted my duplicate `SessionLogStore` port and the whole `outbound/` module; the service is now generic over `AgentSessionLogRepo` directly. To make that work I expanded `agent_session` as you authorized:\n- Added `+ Send` to both port traits' futures — without it a generic consumer can't hold them across an await. Its 5 DB-backed tests still pass, and it's the only implementor besides the new double.\n- Added `testing::InMemoryAgentSessionRepo` behind a `test-utils` feature (mirroring `agent_runtime_protocol`'s pattern), implementing both traits. That's what `agent_fold`'s tests and the example run on.\n\nA nice consequence of dropping the duplicate port: `CommsContext` reads straight off the `AgentSession` record, which already carries `thread_id` and `bot_id` — so callers don't supply the channel or the bot.\n\n18 tests + doctest green, strict clippy clean on both crates. The example now prints both representations; on your real jj-push session the channel side reads as an actual conversation — prose, a `● **Bash**` block with the command and its output, `_Permission requested — Always Allow._`, then the reply.\n\n**One gap I couldn't close honestly:** `AgentSessionLog` carries no `created_at`, even though the table has one. I left rendered timestamps at the epoch rather than invent wall-clock values that would change every read. Threading a frame timestamp through the log model is the fix — small change to `agent_session`, but it touches the row mapping, so I left it for your call.",
